You are tasked with creating a program that parses and extracts information from HTML code containing badge images and their associated URLs. The badge images are represented as `<img>` tags with `src` attributes pointing to shields.io URLs, and the associated URLs are represented as `<a>` tags with `href` attributes. Your program should extract the version number from the `src` attribute of the first `<img>` tag and the download count from the `src` attribute of the second `<img>` tag.

Write a function `parseBadges(html: &str) -> Option<(String, u32)>` that takes an HTML string as input and returns an `Option` containing a tuple with the version number as a `String` and the download count as a `u32`. If the HTML contains the necessary information, the function should return `Some((version, downloads))`, where `version` is the version number extracted from the first `<img>` tag and `downloads` is the download count extracted from the second `<img>` tag. If the information is not found or is in an invalid format, the function should return `None`.

For example, given the following HTML snippet:
```html
<div>
  <a href="https://crates.io/crates/fp-rs">
    <img src="https://img.shields.io/crates/v/fp-rs.svg?style=flat-square" alt="Crates.io version" />
  </a>
  <a href="https://crates.io/crates/fp-rs">
    <img src="https://img.shields.io/crates/d/fp-rs.svg?style=flat-square" alt="Download" />
  </a>
</div>
```
The function `parseBadges` should return `Some(("fp-rs", 0))` because the version number is "fp-rs" and the download count is 0.

### Solution:
```rust
use regex::Regex;

pub fn parseBadges(html: &str) -> Option<(String, u32)> {
    let img_regex = Regex::new(r#"<img src="https://img.shields.io/crates/v/([^"]+).svg\?style=flat-square".*?/>"#).unwrap();
    let a_regex = Regex::new(r#"<a href="https://crates.io/crates/([^"]+)">.*?<img src="https://img.shields.io/crates/d/([^"]+).svg\?style=flat-square".*?/>"#).unwrap();

    if let Some(captures) = img_regex.captures(html) {
        let version = captures.get(1).unwrap().as_str().to_string();
        if let Some(a_captures) = a_regex.captures(html) {
            let downloads = a_captures.get(2).unwrap().as_str().parse::<u32>().unwrap_or(0);
            return Some((version, downloads));
        }
    }
    None
}

fn main() {
    let html = r#"
    <div>
      <a href="https://crates.io/crates/fp-rs">
        <img src="https://img.shields.io/crates/v/fp-rs.svg?style=flat-square" alt="Crates.io version" />
