You are tasked with implementing a function that processes a given JSX code snippet and extracts information about the components and their attributes. JSX (JavaScript XML) is a syntax extension for JavaScript often used with React for describing what the UI should look like. In this problem, you will focus on extracting component names and their attributes from the given JSX code.

Write a function `extractJSXInfo` that takes a string representing JSX code as input and returns an object containing the component names and their attributes. The JSX code will consist of components, attributes, and their values.

For example, given the following JSX code snippet:
```jsx
<Flex direction="column" align="center">
  <Stack spacing={4}>
    <Text fontSize="xl">Hello, World!</Text>
  </Stack>
  <Box rounded="md" shadow="lg" maxW="700px">
    <Image
      src={nativeFeelingSrc}
      alt="native feeling illustration"
      placeholder="blur"
    />
  </Box>
</Flex>
```
The function should return the following object:
```javascript
{
  Flex: { direction: "column", align: "center" },
  Stack: { spacing: 4 },
  Text: { fontSize: "xl" },
  Box: { rounded: "md", shadow: "lg", maxW: "700px" },
  Image: { src: "{nativeFeelingSrc}", alt: "native feeling illustration", placeholder: "blur" }
}
```
Note that the attribute values are represented as strings in the returned object.

Assume the following:
- The JSX code will be well-formed and valid.
- Component names and attribute names will consist of alphanumeric characters and may contain hyphens.
- Attribute values will be enclosed in double quotes and may contain any characters, including spaces and special characters.

### Solution:
```javascript
function extractJSXInfo(jsxCodestring) {
  const jsxInfo = {};
  const regex = /<(\w+)[^>]*>/g;
  const components = jsxCodestring.match(regex);

  if (components) {
    components.forEach((component) => {
      const componentName = component.match(/<(\w+)/)[1];
      const attributes = component.match(/\w+="[^"]*"/g);
      if (attributes) {
        jsxInfo[componentName] = {};
        attributes.forEach((attribute) => {
          const [attrName, attrValue] = attribute.split('=');
          jsxInfo[componentName][attrName] = attrValue.slice(1, -1);
        });
      }
    });
  }
  return jsxInfo;
}
```

The `extractJSXInfo` function uses regular expressions to extract component names and their attributes from the given JSX code. It first matches all the components in the JSX code using a regular expression. Then, for each component, it extracts the component name and its attributes using another set of regular expressions. The function then populates an object with the component names as keys and their attributes as nested objects, and returns this object as the result.

