### Problem:
You are tasked with creating a CSS variable resolver function that can handle cascading variable references. Cascading variables are defined as a fallback mechanism where a variable references another variable, and if the referenced variable is not defined, it falls back to a default value.

Given the following CSS variable declarations:
```
--mdc-dialog-content-ink-color: var(--hcv-text-color-primary);
--hcv-color-fab: var(--racelandshop-fab-color, var(--accent-color));
```

Write a function `resolveCSSVariables` that takes a CSS variable name as input and returns the resolved value. The function should handle cascading variable references and return the final resolved value.

Function Signature: `function resolveCSSVariables(variableName: string): string`

Example:
```javascript
resolveCSSVariables('--mdc-dialog-content-ink-color'); 
// Output: 'var(--hcv-text-color-primary)'

resolveCSSVariables('--hcv-color-fab'); 
// Output: 'var(--racelandshop-fab-color, var(--accent-color))'
```

### Solution:
```javascript
function resolveCSSVariables(variableName) {
  const variableRegex = /var\(([^)]+)\)/; // Regex to match var(--variable-name)
  let currentVariable = `var(${variableName})`; // Start with the input variable

  while (variableRegex.test(currentVariable)) {
    const matches = currentVariable.match(variableRegex); // Extract the inner variable name
    const innerVariable = matches[1].trim();

    // Check if the inner variable is defined
    if (getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue(innerVariable)) {
      currentVariable = currentVariable.replace(variableRegex, getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue(innerVariable));
    } else {
      // If inner variable is not defined, replace with the default value (if present)
      const defaultValue = innerVariable.split(',')[1];
      if (defaultValue) {
        currentVariable = currentVariable.replace(variableRegex, defaultValue.trim());
      } else {
        // If no default value, remove the var() and return the unresolved variable
        currentVariable = currentVariable.replace(variableRegex, innerVariable);
      }
    }
  }

  return currentVariable;
}
```

This `resolveCSSVariables` function uses a while loop to iteratively resolve the cascading variables until no more `var()` references are found. It uses `getComputedStyle` to access the CSS variable values and handles fallback to default values when necessary.

