```

For example, given a CSV file "data.csv" with the following content:
```
Name, Age, City
John, 25, New York
Alice, 30, San Francisco
Bob, 28, Los Angeles
```
The function `parse_csv("data.csv")` should return:
```
Ok(vec![
    vec!["Name".to_string(), "Age".to_string(), "City".to_string()],
    vec!["John".to_string(), "25".to_string(), "New York".to_string()],
    vec!["Alice".to_string(), "30".to_string(), "San Francisco".to_string()],
    vec!["Bob".to_string(), "28".to_string(), "Los Angeles".to_string()]
])
```
If the file does not exist or there is an error in reading the file, the function should return an error with an appropriate message.

### Solution:
```rust
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::{BufReader, BufRead};

fn parse_csv(file_path: &str) -> Result<Vec<Vec<String>>, String> {
    let file = match File::open(file_path) {
        Ok(f) => f,
        Err(_) => return Err("Error opening file".to_string()),
    };

    let reader = BufReader::new(file);
    let mut records: Vec<Vec<String>> = Vec::new();

    for (i, line) in reader.lines().enumerate() {
        let line = match line {
            Ok(l) => l,
            Err(_) => return Err("Error reading line".to_string()),
        };

        let fields: Vec<String> = line.split(',').map(|s| s.trim().to_string()).collect();
        if i == 0 {
            records.push(fields);
        } else {
            if fields.len() != records[0].len() {
                return Err("Inconsistent number of fields in the CSV".to_string());
            }
            records.push(fields);
        }
    }

    Ok(records)
}
```

