```

The function should take a string `sourceCode` representing the content of a C++ source code file and return a vector of strings containing the names of all included header files.

Assumptions:
- The input source code will be well-formed and contain valid `#include` directives.
- The included file names will be enclosed in either double quotes (`"`) or angle brackets (`< >`).
- The included file names will not contain any whitespace characters.

### Solution:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <regex>

std::vector<std::string> extractIncludedFiles(const std::string& sourceCode) {
    std::vector<std::string> includedFiles;
    std::regex includeRegex("#include\\s+([\"<])([^\">]+)([\">])");
    std::smatch match;

    auto it = sourceCode.cbegin();
    while (std::regex_search(it, sourceCode.cend(), match, includeRegex)) {
        includedFiles.push_back(match[2].str());
        it = match.suffix().first;
    }

    return includedFiles;
}

int main() {
