function extractSvgAttributes(svgIconDefinition) {
  const match = svgIconDefinition.match(/<path\s+(.*?)\/>/);
  if (match) {
    const attributesString = match[1];
    const attributes = attributesString.split(/\s+/).reduce((result, attribute) => {
      const [key, value] = attribute.split('=');
      if (key && value) {
        result[key.trim()] = value.replace(/['"]/g, '');
      }
      return result;
    }, {});
    return {
      fill: attributes.fill || '',
      fillRule: attributes.fillRule || '',
      clipRule: attributes.clipRule || '',
      d: attributes.d || ''
    };
  }
  return {};
}
```

This solution uses regular expressions to extract the attributes from the `<path>` element and then processes the extracted attributes to form the desired object.

### Problem:
You are tasked with implementing a function to parse and extract specific information from a given C++ code snippet. The code snippet contains a nested namespace structure, and the goal is to extract the innermost namespace name. The namespace names are separated by the `::` operator, and the innermost namespace is the one closest to the end of the snippet.

Write a function `extractInnermostNamespace` that takes a string representing the C++ code snippet as input and returns the name of the innermost namespace. If no namespace is found, the function should return an empty string.

For example, given the following code snippet:
```
} // namespace RedisProxy
} // namespace NetworkFilters
} // namespace Extensions
```
The innermost namespace is "Extensions", so the function should return "Extensions".

Function Signature: 
```cpp
std::string extractInnermostNamespace(const std::string& codeSnippet);
```

Assumptions:
- The input code snippet is well-formed and contains valid C++ namespace declarations.
- The code snippet may contain other content apart from the namespace declarations, but the function should only consider the namespace declarations for extraction.

### Solution:
```cpp
#include <string>

std::string extractInnermostNamespace(const std::string& codeSnippet) {
    size_t pos = codeSnippet.find_last_of("namespace");
    if (pos != std::string::npos) {
        size_t start = codeSnippet.find_first_of("{", pos);
        size_t end = codeSnippet.find_last_of("}", start);
        if (start != std::string::npos && end != std::string::npos) {
            std::string namespaceBlock = codeSnippet.substr(start, end - start + 1);
            size_t lastNamespacePos = namespaceBlock.find_last_of("namespace");
            if (lastNamespacePos != std::string::npos) {
                size_t namespaceStart = namespaceBlock.find_first_of(" ", lastNamespacePos);
                size_t namespaceEnd = namespaceBlock.find_first_of("{", namespaceStart);
                if (namespaceStart != std::string::npos && namespaceEnd != std::string::npos) {
                    std::string innermostNamespace = namespaceBlock.substr(namespaceStart + 1, namespaceEnd - namespaceStart - 2);
                    return innermostNamespace;
                }
            }
        }
    }
    return "";
}
```

The `extractInnermostNamespace` function first finds the last occurrence of the "namespace" keyword in the code snippet. It then locates the corresponding namespace block by finding the opening and closing curly braces. Within the namespace block, it finds the last occurrence of the "namespace" keyword again to extract the innermost namespace name. If successful, it returns the innermost namespace; otherwise, it returns an empty string.

